{"intent":"Write patch and report","command":"work/tools/finish "humanize.metric printed one digit more than precision when rounding carried into a higher power of ten (9996 -> '10.00 k', 99960 -> '100.0 k', 9.9996 -> '10.00').

In src/humanize/number.py, metric() only handled the carry that reaches 1000 (into the next SI prefix); it never handled a carry into the next power of ten within the same prefix, so the value was formatted with one decimal place too many. I replaced that one-shot block with a loop that (a) still carries into the next prefix when the rounded value reaches 1000 and (b) now also bumps the exponent by one when round(value, digits) crosses 10**(exponent%3+1), re-deriving digits = max(0, precision - exponent%3 - 1) after each carry, so the output never exceeds precision significant figures. The bucket rescale now updates old_bucket so repeated carries work, the pre-scaling input is kept as original_value, and if a carry runs past the largest prefix ('Q', exponent >= 33) it falls back to scientific() as the function already does out of range. I also guarded both scientific fallbacks with max(precision - 1, 0) so precision=0 no longer raises 'Format specifier missing precision'.

Reported cases now give '10.0 k', '100 k', '10.0'; 10.4 k / 104 k / 1.00 kW are unchanged, and all 310 tests plus the doctests pass.""}

